{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"two minutes hate\" (+ unity solidarity together communal collective))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_758826587#6_1527249315","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q836","rank":1,"snippet":"758826587#6_1527249315\" , \"end_char\": 6402.000000 , \"headings\": \"Two Minutes Hate 1984 1984 Two Minutes Hate 1984 Expert Answers Further Reading We’ll help your grades soar \" , \"segment\": \"Further Reading https://www.enotes.com/topics/1984 Last Updated by eNotes Editorial on December 15, 2020 Colin Cavendish-Jones, Ph.D. College Professor, Lawyer M.A. from Oxford University Ph.D. from St. Andrews University Educator since 2019 4,220 answers | Certified Educator Page Citation Share Link In 1984, the <cover>Two Minutes Hate is a collective </cover>act of affirmation in which Party members gather in front of a telescreen to express their hatred of the Party's enemies. For this two minutes, the words of Emmanuel Goldstein, the Enemy of the People and leader of the clandestine opposition which Winston later attempts to join, are broadcast, while his image appears on the telescreen. Orwell remarks that the program of the Two Minutes' Hate varies each day, but Goldstein is always the principal figure. The Two Minutes Hate is important as a plot device in the story because it regularly brings Winston into contact "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"0/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_758826587#6_1527249315","relevance":3,"reason":"The document is specifically about the Two Minutes Hate in 1984 and states that it is a collective act in which Party members gather to express hatred of enemies, directly supporting how hatred unites people under the Party.","learned":"The Two Minutes Hate functions as a collective affirmation: Party members unite by jointly expressing hatred toward Goldstein and other Party enemies."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q836","reason":"The query produced a highly relevant result; continue through this result list for more documents connecting Two Minutes Hate with unity or collective solidarity."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q836"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_758826587#1_1527239448","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q836","rank":2,"snippet":"participate in an intense expression of hatred against enemies of the state. Its overt purpose is to bind Party members in solidarity and collective purpose against those who would undermine or destroy the government of Oceania. Its covert purpose is to allow people to vent their repressed aggressions and frustrations in a socially sanctioned way. These aggressions are caused by the many deprivations and humiliations the inner Party deliberately orchestrates to keep people broken, miserable, and under control. The <cover>Two Minutes Hate also functions as a plot device. In a society in which people are kept alienated and apart from each other—and in which too much interaction with others can potentially be a thought crime—this activity brings people together </cover>as a group. It gives Winston a chance to observe Julia, even though at first, he doesn't even know her name, and to interact as well with O'Brien, albeit fleetingly.\" , \"start_char\": 653.000000 , \"title\": \"Two Minutes Hate 1984 - eNotes.com\" , \"url\": \"https://www.enotes.com/homework-help/what-is-the-two-minutes-hate-in-1984-and-what-is-"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"1/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_758826587#1_1527239448","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage directly answers the information need: it says the Two Minutes Hate's overt purpose is to bind Party members in solidarity and collective purpose against enemies, and that it brings people together as a group.","learned":"Hatred is used by the Party to create solidarity and collective purpose against a shared enemy while also channeling aggression in a controlled, socially sanctioned ritual."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q836","reason":"This query is producing directly relevant grade-3 evidence, so continue to gather more results from the same focused result list."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q836"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_758826587#7_1527251764","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q836","rank":3,"snippet":"even sure that he exists, he finds that he does not need to pretend to hate the figure on the telescreen. After about thirty seconds, he feels a sense of passionate hatred for the figure on the screen and the words he hears, and he joins unaffectedly with the other Party members in their frenzy of hatred. The Party is therefore able to promote orthodoxy and cohesion at the same time. Last Updated by eNotes Editorial on November 30, 2020 marilynn07 Educator since 2009 469 answers | Certified Educator Page Citation Share Link The <cover>Two Minutes Hate is a ritual observance that is designed to use the collective </cover>rage of the people against supposed \"enemies of the Party\" to strengthen the Party's position among the people. The ritual serves to unify the people in the observance of the ritual. The Two Minutes Hate also serves the purpose of religious observance by deifying Big Brother. It serves to channel the rage that persons may feel toward the lack of control over their own lives away from the Party and against purported enemies of the Party. Because Big Brother is proclaimed to be benevolent and good, any enemy is automatically evil and "}}
