{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"1984\" \"hate week\" (+ unity unite solidarity collective cohesion))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_332200854#9_753698706","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q837","rank":1,"snippet":"the Records Department in the Ministry of Truth rewrites history to fit whatever the government claims, and Castro’s Radio Rebelde (and later written publications) always made Cuba’s situation appear better than reality (Infante). Additionally, in order to unify the public in each of these dystopian societies, governments organized rallies designed to promote the ideals of the society. In The Handmaid’s Tale, Atwood describes the Salvagings, which are very similar to the <cover>Hate Week festivities in 1984. In both, a common enemy is presented before large crowds, and chaos ensues as citizens grow increasingly belligerent toward the enemy. In these societies, the governments encourage citizens to watch executions of criminals, and this serves as both a warning and as a means of unity </cover>against a common cause. Similarly, in Castro’s case, millions of Cubans gathered outside the Presidential Palace during Castro’s rise to power in 1960 to approve executions of Batista supporters (Rally for Castro), and this shows how leaders can use mob mentality to their advantage to create a cohesive state. Overall, the use of propaganda in the form of censorship and violent public rallies allow governments to curb their citizens’ independent thinking and control "}}
