{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"1984\" \"common enemy\" (+ hatred hate unity unite))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_332200854#8_753696385","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q838","rank":2,"snippet":"only show us victories, never defeats” (Atwood 83). Similarly, the Party and Castro’s government also censor their media; in Oceania, the Records Department in the Ministry of Truth rewrites history to fit whatever the government claims, and Castro’s Radio Rebelde (and later written publications) always made Cuba’s situation appear better than reality (Infante). Additionally, in order to unify the public in each of these dystopian societies, governments organized rallies designed to promote the ideals of the society. In The Handmaid’s Tale, Atwood describes the Salvagings, which are very similar to the <cover>Hate Week festivities in 1984. In both, a common enemy </cover>is presented before large crowds, and chaos ensues as citizens grow increasingly belligerent toward the enemy.\" , \"start_char\": 7746.000000 , \"title\": \"An Analysis of the Use of Propaganda in the Dystopian Societies Presented in The Handmaid’s Tale by Margaret Atwood, 1984 by George Orwell, and Fidel Castro’s Government in Cuba – Kylie's Blog\" , \"url\": \"https://kyliecblog.wordpress.com/2016/10/06/an-analysis-of-the-use-of-propaganda-in-the-dystopian-societies-presented-in-the-","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_332200854#9_753698706","rank":1,"relevance":3}]}}
