{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"1984\" \"two minutes hate\" (+ control manipulate propaganda) (+ people citizens masses))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_758826587#3_1527243476","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q842","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_758826587#3_1527243476\" , \"end_char\": 3849.000000 , \"headings\": \"Two Minutes Hate 1984 1984<cover>Two Minutes Hate 1984 Expert Answers Further Reading We’ll help your grades soar \" , \"segment\": \"It also shows how the Party uses propaganda to manipulate people. </cover>It reveals how quickly enemies can change, which forces people to engage in doublethink or quickly forget what they once knew in order to align with a new reality that must simultaneously be accepted as what always has been. Last Updated by eNotes Editorial on December 15, 2020 Carroll Khan, M.A. Teacher (K-12) B.A. from Indiana University of Pennsylvania M.A. from Indiana University of Pennsylvania Educator since 2020 351 answers | Certified Educator Page Citation Share Link The Two Minutes Hate in 1984 is a daily ritual where Party members gather in front of a large telescreen and vent their rage and suppressed emotions towards flashing images of Emmanuel Goldstein and enemy soldiers. During the Two Minutes Hate, Winston and his fellow Party members feel compelled to shout, spit, "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"10/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_758826587#3_1527243476","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage is dedicated to the Two Minutes Hate and states that the Party uses propaganda to manipulate people, with Party members gathering to vent rage at Goldstein and enemy soldiers. It supports collective manipulation through hatred, but the snippet does not directly say hatred unites people or creates solidarity/cohesion.","learned":"The Two Minutes Hate is described as Party propaganda that manipulates people by gathering them to vent suppressed rage at Goldstein and enemy soldiers."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q842","reason":"This query may surface additional sources beyond the already strong eNotes evidence, especially passages that connect propaganda, collective hatred, and citizen control more directly to unity."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q842"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1809816257#0_2847491799","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q842","rank":2,"snippet":"the English language. In writing the work, Orwell was influenced and inspired by totalitarian regimes of the time, including Hitler's Nazi Germany and Stalin's Soviet Union. Both regimes glorified their respective leaders as demi-gods and saviors, required the destruction of all individuality in order to promote the Party's needs over the individual's, demanded absolute loyalty from their citizens, and resorted to violence whenever disloyalty was suspected. Moreover, both regimes consistently demonized their enemies, just as the Party and Big Brother do in <cover>1984, through the Two Minutes Hate, Hate Week, and daily mass propaganda. Other parallels include the Thought Police as a reinvention of the Gestapo, NKVD (People'</cover>s Comissariat for Internal Affairs), which orchestrated large scale purges and terror, and the Spies and Youth League as a reinvention of the Hitler Youth and the Little Octoberists, which indoctrinated young people to the Party and encouraged them to report disloyalty observed in their elders, even among family members. The similarities between 1984 's Oceania and Stalin's regime are particularly striking. Like Stalin, the Oceanian government embraces characteristics of both fascist and communist authoritarianism: the former glorifies the wisdom of the leader, and the latter, "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"11/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1809816257#0_2847491799","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage discusses 1984's totalitarian context and notes that the Party demonizes enemies through the Two Minutes Hate, Hate Week, and mass propaganda. It supports hatred as propaganda/control, but it does not directly explain hatred as a tool to unite people.","learned":"Some analyses connect the Two Minutes Hate and Hate Week to demonizing enemies through mass propaganda, but not all explicitly discuss unity or cohesion."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"1984\" \"two minutes hate\" (+ solidarity unity unify unites) (+ enemy enemies goldstein))","reason":"Look for additional sources that explicitly connect the Two Minutes Hate, shared enemies such as Goldstein, and solidarity or unity."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"1984\" \"two minutes hate\" (+ solidarity unity unify unites) (+ enemy enemies goldstein))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_52_1513886989#11_3086078179","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q843","rank":5,"snippet":"that govern their lives. Analysis: Chapters I–III Like the <cover>Two Minutes Hate, the Party’s parading of political enemies through public squares is a demonstration of psychological manipulation. The convoy channels the public’s hatred away from the Party into a political direction that is helpful to the Party. Additionally, the Party’s use of such displays illustrates how war serves to preserve cultural uniformity. War unites the citizens in opposition against some shadowy foreign evil while also making it impossible for its subjects to meet or exchange ideas with citizens from other countries, since the only foreigners in London are prisoners of war. In concert with the Party’s rewriting of history, this policy leaves Oceania’s inhabitants with nothing against which to compare their lives, rendering them unable to challenge the status quo. The opening of Book Two, in which Winston meets Julia and begins the erotic affair he has so deeply desired, commences the main section of the novel and strikes an immediate contrast between the two lovers.\" , \"start_char\": 4923.000000 , \"title\": \"1984: </cover>Book Two: Chapters I–III | SparkNotes\" , \"url","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_758826587#0_1527237636","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_758826587#6_1527249315","rank":2,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_758826587#7_1527251764","rank":3,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_758826587#1_1527239448","rank":4,"relevance":3}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"12/50"}}
