{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_873931060#14_1477673807","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage directly states that in 1984 war is waged upon the people to maintain loyalty, unity against a common enemy, and Party domination. This directly supports hatred/opposition toward a shared enemy as a tool for uniting people, though it is near-duplicate evidence from the same source as the previous result.","learned":"Perpetual war in 1984 maintains loyalty and unity by focusing citizens against a common enemy, reinforcing Party domination."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q853","reason":"The current common-enemy query is yielding direct evidence about unity and Party domination; continue to look for more grade-3 passages, ideally from distinct documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q853"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_731902748#7_1459904439","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q853","rank":3,"snippet":": \"In 1984 , what do these 3 slogans mean: War is Peace, Freedom is Slavery, and Ignorance is Strength? 1984 In <cover>1984, what do these 3 slogans mean: War is Peace, Freedom is Slavery, and Ignorance is Strength? Expert Answers Further Reading We’ll help your grades soar \" , \"segment\": \"Thus, according to the Party, \\\"war is peace\\\" means that they continually \\\"fight\\\" wars in order to keep peace at home. During times of war, nations generally unite. Of course, if the people are focused on a common enemy, they are much less inclined to notice how unhappy they are in their own lives. So they make less trouble for their government. \\\" Freedom is slavery\\\" can be thought of in the same way: the slavery of Party members equals freedom for Party leaders. Finally, \\\"Ignorance is strength\\\" can be read \\\"Your ignorance is our strength\\\", again meaning that the ignorance of the people translates into the strength of the government. It goes directly into the concept of doublethink, which Emmanuel Goldstein </cover>explains through the word \"blackwhite\": But it means also the ability to believe that black is white, and more, to know that black "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"23/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_731902748#7_1459904439","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage directly explains that in 1984 continual war keeps peace at home because nations unite during war and people focused on a common enemy are less likely to notice their misery or oppose the government. This directly supports the idea that hostility toward an enemy is used to unite and control people.","learned":"The Party uses perpetual war and a common enemy to unify citizens, distract them from their own unhappiness, and reduce resistance to the government."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q853","reason":"The common-enemy query continues to produce direct grade-3 evidence about unity, loyalty, and Party domination; continue for more relevant results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q853"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_332200854#3_753681491","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q853","rank":4,"snippet":"The Party indoctrinates the citizens to believe that “all subsequent crimes against the Party… sprang directly out of [Goldstein’s] teaching” (Orwell 12), and consequently schedules Two Minute Hates (Orwell 2 Minutes of Hate) each day that feature videos of <cover>Goldstein speaking and the mobs of Party members expressing their deep hate of his teachings and their loyalty to the Party. In fact, the Hates work the citizens into such a nationalistic frenzy that “before the hate [s] had proceeded for thirty seconds, uncontrollable exclamations of rage were breaking out from half the people in the room” (Orwell 13). These overt forms of propaganda serve to unite the citizens against a common enemy and strengthen the sentiments of patriotism among Party members.\" , \"start_char\": 2935.000000 , \"title\": \"An Analysis of the Use of Propaganda in the Dystopian Societies Presented in The Handmaid’s Tale by Margaret Atwood, 1984 </cover>by George Orwell, and Fidel Castro’s Government in Cuba – Kylie's Blog\" , \"url\": \"https://kyliecblog.wordpress.com/2016/10/06/an-analysis-of-the-use-of-propaganda-in-the-dystopian-societies-presented-in-"}}
