{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_19_1966159771#1_3226872545","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage discusses 1984's telescreens broadcasting propaganda, patriotic material, and the Two Minutes Hate featuring Emmanuel Goldstein, whom citizens are trained to oppose. It supports the use of propaganda and hatred to heighten patriotism, but it is not dedicated to explaining hatred as a tool to unite people.","learned":"Telescreens help broadcast patriotic propaganda and the Two Minutes Hate, training citizens to direct opposition toward Goldstein."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"1984\" \"war is peace\" \"common enemy\" (+ unite united unity))","reason":"Search for more direct evidence about the Party slogan 'War is Peace' and the common-enemy mechanism, which has already proven productive for showing hatred/opposition used to unite people in 1984."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"1984\" \"war is peace\" \"common enemy\" (+ unite united unity))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_731902748#6_1459902809","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q855","rank":1,"snippet":"sharing information. Please try again later. Watch on 0:00 0:00 0:00 / 12:42 Live • MaudlinStreet Educator since 2009 857 answers | Certified Educator Page Citation Share Link This novel is constructed through paradox and contrasts. These three slogans of the Party reflect these aspects, in that the way the Party controls Oceania is actually the opposite of the propaganda it produces. Thus, according to the Party, \"war is peace\" means that they continually \"fight\" wars in order to keep peace at home. During times of war, nations generally <cover>unite. Of course, if the people are focused on a common enemy, they are much less inclined to notice how unhappy they are in their own lives. So they make less trouble for their government. \" Freedom is slavery\" can be thought of in the same way:\" , \"start_char\": 3843.000000 , \"title\": \"In 1984, what do these 3 slogans mean: War is Peace, </cover>Freedom is Slavery, and Ignorance is Strength?  - eNotes.com\" , \"url\": \"https://www.enotes.com/homework-help/in-1984-what-do-these-3-slogans-mean-war-is-peace-201861\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"29/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_731902748#6_1459902809","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage directly explains that in 1984 the Party's slogan 'War is Peace' works because war makes nations unite, and people focused on a common enemy are less likely to notice their own misery or trouble the government. This directly supports hatred/opposition toward enemies as a unifying tool.","learned":"The Party uses war and common enemies to unite citizens, distract them from dissatisfaction, and keep them compliant."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q855","reason":"The War is Peace/common-enemy query is producing direct evidence about unity through opposition to enemies; continue for more grade-3 results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q855"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_12_822518523#2_1573293244","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q855","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_12_822518523#2_1573293244\" , \"end_char\": 1156.000000 , \"headings\": \" War is Peace. Freedom is Slavery. Ignorance is Strength. By: Monica Castro Leave a Reply Cancel reply \" , \"segment\": \"Freedom Is Slavery. Ignorance is Strength.” These are three slogans of the Party, Ingsoc. But what do they mean? “<cover>War is Peace” means that while Oceania is at war with other countries, the people will maintain united because they have a common enemy. Therefore, by keeping war, people don’t have enemies inside the country, because they are busy fighting enemies outside the country. In 1984, </cover>the war isn’t even necessary, but is kept as a means to control the people. The contradiction with this quote is that obviously war and peace are opposites. People can’t have peace when they feel threatened by a war, even if it is a foreign war. In 1984, people feel threatened by rocket bombs and Eurasian soldiers, when the real threat is the Party itself.\" , \"start_char\": 405.000000 ,"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"30/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_12_822518523#2_1573293244","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage directly explains that in 1984 'War is Peace' means Oceania's people stay united because they have a common external enemy, and that war is maintained as a way to control them. This directly supports hostility toward enemies as a unifying tool, though it appears to be a brief student/blog explanation.","learned":"The Party keeps Oceania at war so citizens unite around a common outside enemy instead of recognizing the Party as the real threat."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q855","reason":"The War is Peace/common-enemy query is yielding direct evidence about unity through external enemies; continue while it remains productive."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q855"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_52_1513770716#4_3085807997","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q855","rank":4,"snippet":"of Truth, as Winston observes in Book One, Chapter I. Because it is introduced so early in the novel, this creed serves as the reader’s first introduction to the idea of doublethink. By weakening the independence and strength of individuals’ minds and forcing them to live in a constant state of propaganda-induced fear, the Party is able to force its subjects to accept anything it decrees, even if it is entirely illogical—for instance, the Ministry of Peace is in charge of waging war, the Ministry of Love is in charge of political torture, and the Ministry of Truth is in charge of doctoring history books to reflect the Party’s ideology. That the national slogan of Oceania is equally contradictory is an important testament to the power of the Party’s mass campaign of psychological control. In theory, the Party is able to maintain that “<cover>War Is Peace” because having a common enemy keeps the people of Oceania united. “\" , \"start_char\": 1769.000000 , \"title\": \"1984: </cover>Important Quotes Explained | SparkNotes\" , \"url\": \"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/1984/quotes/\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_731902748#7_1459904439","rank":3,"relevance":3}]}}
