{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_53_403399832#1_802844933","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage directly explains that in 1984 a common enemy creates camaraderie and that the government fires people up through the Two Minutes Hate and Hate Week rather than emotions like love or tranquility. This directly supports hatred as a tool for uniting people.","learned":"The Party uses common enemies and organized hate rituals like Two Minutes Hate and Hate Week to create camaraderie and emotionally mobilize citizens."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q860","reason":"The Hate Week/common-enemy query has produced a direct nonduplicate grade-3 result after skipped known results; continue to find more evidence about mass hatred creating unity."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q860"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_53_403399832#0_802842809","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q860","rank":4,"snippet":"children to believe nothing is more important than The Party, quality of life, the sharing of ideas, and simple things like freedom all become repressed. The government continues to maintain control of its people by making them think they are constantly at war, but no side ever wins in this war. When there is a <cover>common enemy, there is camaraderie. In addition, rather than focusing on emotions like love and tranquility, the government fires people up through exercises such as Two Minutes Hate, and Hate Week. While there is no resolution to this absolutist tyranny in the novel, it does lay out the dangers of allowing a government, political party, or dictator gain too much control over citizens’ privacy and lives. Propaganda The media reports in Oceania are all propaganda, because they are all controlled by the government. For example, Winston is sent a few short missives that come down a mysterious pneumatic tube.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"Illustrating Themes in 1984 </cover>by George Orwell\" , \"url\": \"https://www.storyboardthat.com/lesson-plans/1984-by-george-orwell/themes-symbols-motifs\" }"}}
